# <%= current_page.data.title %>

## Overview

Container Platform 3 (CP3) runs several of the cluster's components as an
AWS-managed service through EKS Auto Mode — Karpenter (compute/scaling), the
EBS CSI driver (block storage), the load balancer controller, and VPC CNI (IP
address management). Their logs are delivered to
[Amazon CloudWatch Logs](https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/) so you can see what
these managed components are doing — for example, why a node did or did not
scale. A copy is also archived to S3 for long-term storage.

These are the platform's **managed-component** logs. They are separate from:

- your **application** logs (searchable in OpenSearch — see
  [Searching logs in OpenSearch](opensearch-log-search)), and
- the EKS **control plane** logs (api, audit, authenticator, and so on).

> This page is a developer and platform-engineer guide. The Terraform that
> enables and delivers these logs lives in
> [modernisation-platform-environments](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform-environments)
> under `terraform/environments/cloud-platform/cluster` (`eks-cluster.tf` for
> CloudWatch, `auto-mode-logs-s3.tf` for S3), and the wider logging design is in
> [ADR-017](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/cloud-platform/blob/main/architecture-decision-record/cp30/ADR-017-logging-and-log-management.md).

## What is captured

All four Auto Mode component log types are enabled on every cluster. You do not
need to configure anything.

| Log type | Component | Useful for |
|---|---|---|
| `AUTO_MODE_COMPUTE_LOGS` | Karpenter | Why nodes scaled up/down; scheduling decisions |
| `AUTO_MODE_BLOCK_STORAGE_LOGS` | EBS CSI driver | Volume provisioning, attach/mount problems |
| `AUTO_MODE_LOAD_BALANCING_LOGS` | Load balancer controller | Why a load balancer / target group is not coming up |
| `AUTO_MODE_IPAM_LOGS` | VPC CNI | IP address exhaustion, ENI problems |

## Where to find them

### CloudWatch Logs (last 30 days)

Each log type goes to its own log group, per cluster:

```
/aws/eks/<cluster-name>/vendedlogs/AUTO_MODE_<COMPONENT>_LOGS
```

For example, the Karpenter logs for a cluster called `cp-2110-1243` are in
`/aws/eks/cp-2110-1243/vendedlogs/AUTO_MODE_COMPUTE_LOGS`. Logs are kept in
CloudWatch for **30 days**. This is the short-term operational tier for
troubleshooting.

In the AWS console, go to **CloudWatch → Log groups** and filter on
`/aws/eks/<cluster-name>/vendedlogs/`, or query the groups with CloudWatch Logs
Insights. From the CLI:

```
aws logs tail /aws/eks/<cluster-name>/vendedlogs/AUTO_MODE_COMPUTE_LOGS \
  --region eu-west-2 --since 1h
```

The groups are **per cluster**, so an ephemeral development cluster has its own
set. Access uses your normal AWS console / CloudWatch permissions — no
OpenSearch access needed.

### S3 (long-term archive)

The same four log types are also delivered to the cluster environment's log
archive bucket (`<terraform-workspace>-fluentbit`), under an `auto-mode/`
prefix with one folder per component (for example `auto-mode/compute/`). This
is the same bucket the Fluent Bit pipeline writes application logs to, under a
separate `logs/` prefix. Use the S3 archive for anything older than 30 days.

## Reading the logs

The four types come in different formats: Karpenter, the load balancer
controller and VPC CNI emit structured JSON; the EBS CSI driver emits klog-style
text wrapped in JSON (the detail is in the message string). Each line carries the
source and a timestamp, so you can line up events across components for a single
cluster action — for example, a scale-up showing in both compute and IPAM.

## How it works

Auto Mode logging is not a single cluster setting. It uses CloudWatch Vended
Logs, and for each log type three resources are wired together: a **delivery
source** (the log type on the cluster), a **delivery destination** (the log
group or the S3 bucket), and a **delivery** joining the two. Each source has two
deliveries, one to CloudWatch and one to S3. A resource policy per cluster,
scoped to `/aws/eks/<cluster-name>/vendedlogs/*`, lets the delivery service
write to that cluster's log groups.

### One log type at a time

Enabling a log type triggers an EKS cluster update, and EKS allows only one such
update at a time. If all four were turned on at once they would collide and
three would fail. So the four are enabled one after another: before each one, a
check waits until the cluster has no update in progress. This adapts to how
long the cluster actually takes rather than using a fixed wait. The check lives
in `terraform/environments/cloud-platform/cluster/scripts/auto_mode_gate_check.sh`.

This runs automatically during a cluster build and adds a few minutes to it. A
slow logging step is expected, not a fault.

### Volume and cost

Volume is small — a few MB per cluster per month across all four types on a
quiet cluster, more when the cluster is busy — at CloudWatch's reduced "vended
logs" rate.

### Argo CD capability logs

The managed Argo CD capability supports the same delivery mechanism (log types
`EKS_CAPABILITY_ARGOCD_*`), but the platform Terraform does not currently
enable it, so Argo CD capability logs are not collected by default.

## Troubleshooting

- **A log group is empty.** Some components only log when something happens
  (for example, load-balancing logs appear when a load balancer is created or
  changes). An idle component produces little. Trigger the relevant activity and
  check again.
- **A cluster deploy failed on the logging step with a conflict error.** This is
  the "one update at a time" limit above. A re-run normally clears it once the
  cluster has settled. If it recurs, check the cluster's update history in the
  EKS console for a stuck update.

